# Cloudflare Pages redirects.
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/redirects/
#
# Note: do NOT add trailing-slash canonicalisation here. Cloudflare Pages
# already serves /staff/index.html transparently; any /staff/ -> /staff
# rule loops with Cloudflare's own /staff -> /staff/ behaviour and triggers
# ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS.

# Apple Pay / Google Pay domain verification. Stripe (and Apple) fetch this
# well-known file to verify the domain; we point it at Stripe's canonical hosted
# copy so it never goes stale. Same file for every shop/domain.
/.well-known/apple-developer-merchantid-domain-association  https://stripe.com/files/apple-pay/apple-developer-merchantid-domain-association  302

# Canonical host: www.ricosyork.co.uk -> ricosyork.co.uk (apex).
# This keeps the origin consistent so getAddress.io's domain-locked
# autocomplete token (registered against the apex) matches every request.
https://www.ricosyork.co.uk/*  https://ricosyork.co.uk/:splat  301

# Canonical host: www.bigbiteseasingwold.co.uk -> bigbiteseasingwold.co.uk (apex).
# Both are attached to the Big Bites Pages project, so without this they'd serve
# identical pages on two hosts and split the SEO signal. The apex is what
# config.business.domain names and what the till's Restaurant ID resolves to, so
# the apex must be the one that does NOT redirect — a 301 in front of /api would
# drop POST bodies and break the till. Matches only this host, so it's a no-op on
# every other shop's deploy.
https://www.bigbiteseasingwold.co.uk/*  https://bigbiteseasingwold.co.uk/:splat  301

# Rico's .com is a redirect-only domain — send it (and its www) to the canonical
# .co.uk so there's ONE address for SEO. Matches only ricosyork.com, so it's a
# no-op on the Food Station deploy. (Needs ricosyork.com + www.ricosyork.com added
# as custom domains on the Rico's Pages project for these to take effect.)
https://ricosyork.com/*      https://ricosyork.co.uk/:splat  301
https://www.ricosyork.com/*  https://ricosyork.co.uk/:splat  301

# Favicon at the conventional root path. Every page already declares
# <link rel="icon" href="/icon.png"> (injected by build-shop.js), but browsers,
# feed readers and link-preview bots still request /favicon.ico blind, and a 404
# there costs the shop its icon in those places. Points at the same file the
# link tag names, so there is only ever one favicon to keep in sync.
# On a shop with no icon.png the build doesn't emit one and this 302s to a 404 —
# same outcome as the bare 404 it replaces, so it is safe everywhere.
/favicon.ico  /icon.png  302

# Friendly URLs.
/menu     /order   302
/book     /order   302
